# 11. Step 7 — Discover ECR Values

Set the intended AWS region and repository.

Example:

```bash
export AWS_REGION="eu-central-1"
export ECR_REPOSITORY="devsecops-webapp"
```

Get AWS account ID:

```bash
export AWS_ACCOUNT_ID="$(aws sts get-caller-identity \
  --query Account \
  --output text)"
```

Build registry URI:

```bash
export ECR_REGISTRY="${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}.dkr.ecr.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com"
```

Build repository URI:

```bash
export ECR_REPO="${ECR_REGISTRY}/${ECR_REPOSITORY}"
```

Check:

```bash
echo "AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=$AWS_ACCOUNT_ID"
echo "ECR_REGISTRY=$ECR_REGISTRY"
echo "ECR_REPO=$ECR_REPO"
```

Verify repository:

```bash
aws ecr describe-repositories \
  --region "$AWS_REGION" \
  --repository-names "$ECR_REPOSITORY"
```

---

# 12. Step 8 — Authenticate Docker to ECR

Use:

```bash
aws ecr get-login-password \
  --region "$AWS_REGION" \
| docker login \
  --username AWS \
  --password-stdin "$ECR_REGISTRY"
```

Amazon ECR authorization tokens are temporary.

At the time this README was prepared, AWS documents that ECR authorization tokens are valid for **12 hours**.

This matters later for GKE image pulling.

Do not design the final platform around manually creating one permanent Kubernetes ECR secret.

