# 13. Step 9 — Tag the Image for ECR

Current local image:

```text
devsecops-webapp:manual-001
```

Tag it:

```bash
docker tag \
  "${APP_NAME}:${IMAGE_TAG}" \
  "${ECR_REPO}:${IMAGE_TAG}"
```

Check:

```bash
docker images
```

---

# 14. Step 10 — Push the Image to ECR

Push:

```bash
docker push "${ECR_REPO}:${IMAGE_TAG}"
```

Verify:

```bash
aws ecr describe-images \
  --region "$AWS_REGION" \
  --repository-name "$ECR_REPOSITORY"
```

---

# 15. Do Not Use `latest` as the Final Deployment Strategy

Avoid final production manifests like:

```yaml
image: example/image:latest
```

The tag `latest` does not tell you exactly which image content should run.

Prefer an immutable release identity such as a Git commit SHA.

Example CI tag:

```bash
IMAGE_TAG="$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
```

Then:

```text
devsecops-webapp:a91c73e
```

Even better for final GitOps desired state:

```text
repository@sha256:<digest>
```

A digest identifies the exact image content.

---

# 16. Step 11 — Retrieve the ECR Image Digest

After pushing:

```bash
export IMAGE_DIGEST="$(aws ecr describe-images \
  --region "$AWS_REGION" \
  --repository-name "$ECR_REPOSITORY" \
  --image-ids imageTag="$IMAGE_TAG" \
  --query 'imageDetails[0].imageDigest' \
  --output text)"
```

Check:

```bash
echo "$IMAGE_DIGEST"
```

Expected format:

```text
sha256:...
```

Build immutable image reference:

```bash
export IMAGE_REF="${ECR_REPO}@${IMAGE_DIGEST}"
```

Check:

```bash
echo "$IMAGE_REF"
```

This is the type of reference the GitOps repository should eventually store.

