# 27. Step 19 — AWS ECR Pull Authentication

This is one of the most important cross-cloud issues in this architecture.

The worker nodes are on GCP GKE while the private registry is AWS ECR.

For a **temporary manual test**, we can create a Kubernetes image pull secret.

Do not commit the generated secret to Git.

Set:

```bash
export AWS_REGION="eu-central-1"
export AWS_ACCOUNT_ID="$(aws sts get-caller-identity \
  --query Account \
  --output text)"
export ECR_REGISTRY="${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}.dkr.ecr.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com"
```

Retrieve a temporary password:

```bash
export ECR_PASSWORD="$(aws ecr get-login-password \
  --region "$AWS_REGION")"
```

Create secret in staging:

```bash
kubectl create secret docker-registry ecr-registry \
  --namespace staging \
  --docker-server="$ECR_REGISTRY" \
  --docker-username="AWS" \
  --docker-password="$ECR_PASSWORD"
```

Then remove the password from the shell variable:

```bash
unset ECR_PASSWORD
```

Verify only metadata:

```bash
kubectl get secret ecr-registry \
  -n staging
```

Do not print or decode the secret unnecessarily.

---

# 28. Attach `imagePullSecrets`

In the Deployment Pod template:

```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      imagePullSecrets:
        - name: ecr-registry
```

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: webapp
  namespace: staging

spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: webapp

      imagePullSecrets:
        - name: ecr-registry

      containers:
        - name: webapp
          image: ACCOUNT.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/devsecops-webapp@sha256:...
```

The secret must exist in the **same namespace** as the Pod.

---

# 29. ECR Authentication Warning

The manual `ecr-registry` secret is **not the final production solution**.

ECR authorization tokens expire.

Therefore this architecture has a real lifecycle problem:

```text
ECR token created
     |
     v
Kubernetes Secret
     |
     v
token expires
     |
     v
existing cached images may continue to run
but
new pulls can fail
```

This becomes particularly important when:

```text
a new node is created
a Pod is rescheduled
a new Deployment rolls out
an image is not cached
```

We will later implement a proper short-lived/federated or securely refreshed authentication design as part of the Vault/cloud identity phase.

Do not put a permanent AWS access key into a Kubernetes YAML file.

