Check EndpointSlices:

```bash
kubectl get endpointslices \
  -n staging
```

If the Service has no backend endpoints, compare Pod labels:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n staging \
  --show-labels
```

with Service selector:

```bash
kubectl get service webapp \
  -n staging \
  -o yaml
```

The Service selector must match the Pod labels.

---

# 42. Step 28 — Basic Rollback

Check rollout history:

```bash
kubectl rollout history \
  deployment/webapp \
  -n staging
```

Rollback:

```bash
kubectl rollout undo \
  deployment/webapp \
  -n staging
```

Then:

```bash
kubectl rollout status \
  deployment/webapp \
  -n staging
```

In the final GitOps model, rollback normally means reverting desired state in Git and letting Argo CD reconcile it.

---

# 43. Step 29 — Staging Before Production

Do not deploy a brand-new image directly to production.

Desired flow:

```text
Build image once
      |
      v
ECR image digest
      |
      v
Staging
      |
      +-- functional validation
      +-- security validation
      +-- DAST
      |
      v
Promote the SAME digest
      |
      v
Production
```

Important:

```text
Do not rebuild a different image for production.
```

Promote the image that was actually tested.

---

# 44. Step 30 — OWASP ZAP Later

Once staging has a reachable URL:

```text
staging endpoint
      |
      v
OWASP ZAP
      |
      v
DAST findings
      |
      v
DefectDojo
```

Only scan systems you own or are authorized to test.

---

# 45. Step 31 — Kubernetes NetworkPolicy

Dataplane V2 is already enabled in the GKE design.

That gives us the network policy enforcement capability.

But Dataplane V2 by itself does not mean all Pod traffic is automatically isolated.

Later we should introduce policies such as:

```text
default deny
    |
    +-- explicitly allow required traffic
```

NetworkPolicy should be introduced carefully after we understand the actual traffic flows.

---

# 46. Step 32 — Secrets

Do not put application secrets directly in Git like:

```yaml
env:
  - name: DB_PASSWORD
    value: "super-secret"
```

Even this:

```yaml
kind: Secret
data:
  password: <base64>
```

is **not encryption** by itself.

Base64 is encoding.

The intended project direction is:

```text
HashiCorp Vault
     |
     v
controlled secret delivery
     |
     v
Kubernetes workload
```

We will design that separately instead of mixing long-lived secrets into application manifests.

---

# 47. Step 33 — Kubernetes Security Baseline

Before production, review every Deployment for:

```text
[ ] non-root where supported
[ ] no privileged container
[ ] no hostPID unless explicitly required
[ ] no hostNetwork unless explicitly required
[ ] no hostPath unless explicitly required
[ ] drop unnecessary capabilities
[ ] no privilege escalation
[ ] resource requests
[ ] resource limits
[ ] readiness probe
[ ] liveness/startup probe
[ ] dedicated ServiceAccount
[ ] immutable image digest
[ ] no embedded secrets
```

Some platform security tools such as Falco may legitimately require more host access than an ordinary application.

Treat those as explicit security exceptions, not defaults.

---

# 48. Step 34 — Check What Kubernetes Actually Created

Useful commands:

```bash
kubectl get all -n staging
kubectl get deployments -n staging
kubectl get replicasets -n staging
kubectl get pods -n staging -o wide
kubectl get services -n staging
kubectl get serviceaccounts -n staging
kubectl get configmaps -n staging
kubectl get secrets -n staging
```

Do not print secret values unless you actually need them for debugging.

---

# 49. Step 35 — Understand the Kubernetes Relationship

This relationship is fundamental:

```text
Deployment
    |
    v
ReplicaSet
    |
    v
Pods
```

And separately:

```text
Service
   |
   | label selector
   v
Pods
```

A Service does not "contain" the Deployment.

It finds Pods through labels.

---

# 50. Step 36 — Check Image Running in the Cluster

See the configured image:

```bash
kubectl get deployment webapp \
  -n staging \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].image}'
echo
```

Inspect actual Pod image IDs:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n staging \
  -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{" => "}{.status.containerStatuses[*].imageID}{"\n"}{end}'
```

This is useful to verify that Kubernetes is running the expected image digest.

---

# 51. Step 37 — Do Not Edit Live Resources as the Normal Workflow

Avoid making normal changes with:

```bash
kubectl edit deployment webapp
```

Why?

Because then:

```text
Git manifest
    !=
Live Kubernetes state
```

That is configuration drift.

During debugging it can sometimes be useful, but the final answer must be moved back into version-controlled configuration.

---

# 52. Step 38 — Transition to GitOps

Once manual deployment works, stop treating `kubectl apply` as the normal CD mechanism.

Create the GitOps repository structure.

Example:

```text
webapp-gitops/
|
+-- base/
|   |
|   +-- deployment.yaml
|   +-- service.yaml
|   +-- serviceaccount.yaml
|
+-- overlays/
    |
    +-- staging/
    |
    +-- production/
```

The exact organization can use plain YAML, Kustomize, or Helm depending on the project's final choice.

Do not introduce multiple templating systems unless they solve a real requirement.

---

# 53. Final Image Promotion Model

Recommended model:

```text
Source commit
   |
   v
Jenkins build
   |
   v
Image pushed to ECR
   |
   v
sha256:ABC
   |
   +-------------------------+
   |                         |
   v                         v
staging Git config      later production config
sha256:ABC              sha256:ABC
```

The artifact remains unchanged.

Only deployment promotion changes.

---

# 54. Jenkins Responsibilities Later

Jenkins should eventually perform steps such as:

```text
Checkout
   |
Gitleaks
   |
SAST
   |
Dependency / SCA scan
   |
Docker build
   |
Trivy image scan
   |
SBOM
   |
Optional signing / attestation
   |
Push to ECR
   |
Update GitOps repository with image digest
```

Jenkins should not become the Kubernetes control plane administrator.

---

# 55. Argo CD Responsibilities Later

Argo CD watches the GitOps repository.

Concept:

```text
Git desired state
       |
       v
Argo CD
       |
compare
       |
       +----> GKE actual state
       |
reconcile
```

This gives us a cleaner separation:

```text
CI = Jenkins
CD = Argo CD
```

